  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "CHANGED\n\t\t\t* A test that provides When must claim something. A bare Then() asserts nothing, not even that the act completed, and now fails the test at teardown; so does Then(subject) or And(subject) with nothing asserted on it. State a completed act with Then().Completes().\n\t\t\t* The trainwreck error names the expression and spells out the rewrite: \"Hand over the root and chain the rest after it: And(Result).Length\".\n\t\t\t* A value read before the pipeline was arranged now fails the test when an arrangement replaces it.\n\t\t\t* A lambda handed to Then(subject) or And(subject) is refused: running the pipeline does not affect it and a delegate has nothing to assert on.\n\t\t\t* A null reads as the type it stands for, wherever it appears: a cast over null renders \"null ILogger\" rather than \"(ILogger)null\".\n\t\t\t* Tap is available on a mock sequence: First() and AndNext() now take a tap\n\t\t\t* A PROTECTED member can be mocked with ThatProtected<ReturnType>(methodName).Returns(...).\n\t\t\t* Both DoesNotThrow forms are obsolete. An act either completes or throws, so Then().Completes() states the fact positively and renders \"completes\".\n\t\t\t* Subject's constructor parameter defaults are honored. Building the subject, TSpec fills such a parameter only if it is given or constrained from what the test arranged\n\t\t\tFIXED\n\t\t\t* ReturnsDefault() on a call that answers with nothing no longer fails. The default of nothing is nothing, so it now states what Returns() states.\n\t\t\t* Two taps on one mocked call both run, in the order they were written, and the specification states both.",
